<p>President Trump warned Iran on Sunday to move quickly toward a deal with the United States as negotiations to end the U.S.-Israeli war with Iran remained stalled. Iranian state-linked media said Washington had offered “no tangible concessions” in its latest response to Tehran’s proposed negotiating agenda. An Israeli official said Trump spoke with Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu about Iran before Netanyahu convened a limited security meeting. Axios reported that Trump was expected to meet his top national security team on Tuesday to discuss military options. Iranian reports described a U.S. five-point proposal that Tehran viewed as demanding nuclear concessions while rejecting key financial relief. The war began when U.S. and Israeli forces launched major strikes on Iran on Feb. 28, and Washington and Tehran agreed to a truce on April 8, according to accounts cited in the reports. Reports described continued shipping disruptions in the Strait of Hormuz and cross-border fire on the Lebanon front despite ceasefire efforts.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/fHSesEG-UFVujNzo_V2miCQ1a50-5NDZKSF5nzfplm5Q3Hy9vTLAiIldmp97wUphHXGayyhasGUdyzeuvU4IH4ypFhs5NBpuHDIKNzVk7w' alt='US President Donald Trump.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>“Calm before the storm”: Trump also posted an AI-generated image tied to Iranian-flagged naval vessels with the phrase.</li><li>Shipping controls: Iranian officials said Tehran was preparing a mechanism to regulate Strait of Hormuz traffic, including a designated route and service charges for approved commercial vessels.</li><li>Military readiness: Haaretz cited a New York Times report saying the U.S. had used the bombing pause to rearm warships and attack planes in the region.</li><li>Iranian demands: Iranian reports said Tehran sought an end to hostilities on multiple fronts, sanctions relief and removal of restrictions affecting its ports and shipping.</li></ul><blockquote>For Iran, the Clock is Ticking, and they better g